<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visayas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Visayan Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Visayas are the central group of islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, including Panay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohol">Bohol</a>, Leyte and Samar. Colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from the 1560s, their coastal settlements suffered raids by fleets from the Sulu zone for more than two centuries, which led to the building of watchtowers and fortified churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visayas.jpg" alt="Visayas" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JL 09 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visayas_Red.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Visayas are one of the three principal geographical divisions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>. They lie in the central part of the archipelago, mostly around the Visayan Sea, which is also considered the northeast extremity of the Sulu Sea. The region has a land area of 71,503 km2 and consists of seven large and several hundred smaller islands, among them Guimaras, Biliran, Siquijor, Panaon and Bantayan. Palawan, Romblon and Masbate may also be included, since their populations identify as Visayan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The origin of the name is unknown. A possible first documented use is by the Song-era Chinese maritime official Zhao Rugua, who wrote of the Pi-sho-ye raiding the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a> in the late 12th century. The Spanish first called the Visayans Pintados, the painted ones, for their full-body tattooing. In his Sucesos de las Islas Filipinas of 1609, Antonio de Morga described them as skilful in navigation and eager for war and raids for plunder, which they called mangubas.</p><p>After the Magellan expedition was defeated at the Battle of Mactan by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapulapu">Lapu-Lapu</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> to colonise the islands. Many of the local kingdoms began to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and Spanish and Mexican conquistadors received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>, chiefly on Panay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>; Cristóbal Sánchez, for example, held about 3000 Cebuano tributaries. Resistance followed, including the Tamblot Uprising of 1621 to 1622 and the Bohol Rebellion led by Francisco Dagohoy, which lasted from 1744 to 1829. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> the short-lived Federal State of the Visayas took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iloilo-city">Iloilo City</a> as its capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Visayas are divided into four administrative regions, Western Visayas, Central Visayas, Eastern Visayas and the Negros Island Region, with sixteen provinces. Cebu City, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacolod">Bacolod</a> and Iloilo are among the largest cities. The main languages are Cebuano, Hiligaynon and Waray, with Aklanon, Kinaray-a and Capiznon also widely spoken; Cebuano alone has about 20 million speakers. The Sinulog festival in Cebu City, the Ati-Atihan in Kalibo and the Dinagyang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iloilo-city">Iloilo City</a>, all held in January, honour the Santo Niño.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Visayas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…met by slave raiding: Iranun and Balangingi Sama fleets based in the Sulu zone seized, by his estimates, thousands of captives a year from the <b>Visayas</b>, Luzon, the Moluccas, Sulawesi and the Malay world, many of whom were set to fishing and collecting marine produce or sold onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Revolutionary forces under General Martin Delgado besieged Iloilo, and on 25 December 1898 the Spanish garrison surrendered, leaving the Federal State of the <b>Visayas</b> in control.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-the-visayas">Federal State of the Visayas</a></li></ul></section>
